Recommends: homelab-api, homelab-postfix, homelab-dovecot, homelab-webproxy, homelab-audit, homelab-agent
Description: Invite mechanism backend + public acceptance page for the homelab-* ecosystem
 Owns the whole invite lifecycle as one singleton service, the only
 process in this fleet holding credentials to the `invite` Postgres
 schema (token issuance, per-sender quota/dedup enforcement, and the
 public one-time-use acceptance page an invitee actually clicks into).
 Fronted two ways from one process: /internal/v1/invites/* via
 homelab-api's own /api/v1/invites/* gateway route (JWT-authenticated,
 same as homelab-domain-admin/homelab-mailbridge), and /invite/* on its
 own public webproxy vhost (unauthenticated -- see README.md's
 "Deployment" section).
 .
 Deliberately never writes to api.users itself: creating the actual
 login-capable account is homelab-api's own job (an extended
 /api/v1/auth/register, gated behind config auth.require_invite),
 reusing its already-proven Argon2id hashing rather than duplicating
 security-critical code in a second, public-facing codebase. This
 service's own role is validating the invite token was real and
 unused, atomically marking it consumed via a server-to-server call
 homelab-api makes into /internal/v1/invites/consume (system_agent
 role required, checked via introspect() -- see App.pm).
 .
 Sends its own mail (the invite itself for CLI-initiated invites, and
 the "your account is ready" welcome email either way) as a real,
 dedicated api.users mailbox identity over authenticated SMTP
 submission -- not an unauthenticated local relay, which
 homelab-postfix's own anti-relay policy correctly rejects for
 external recipients anyway. See `homelab-cli admin users
 create-service-account`.
 .
 The companion package homelab-roundcube-invite adds a "Send Invite"
 button to an already-installed homelab-roundcube, calling this
 service's /api/v1/invites/* through the same gateway with the logged-
 in user's own JWT -- it holds no database credential of its own.
